          "summary": "list_orders calls get_order for each row returned by list_by_tenant. Since list_by_tenant likely returns full order rows, get_order performs redundant database fetches (N+1 pattern). Even if list_by_tenant returned only IDs, this would be a classic N+1 query performance issue."
        },
        {
          "id": "crash_on_missing_order",
          "severity": "medium",
          "summary": "get_order raises KeyError if an order is not found or has a tenant mismatch. If this occurs during the list_orders loop (e.g., due to a race condition where an order is deleted), the entire list_orders operation will crash instead of returning a partial list."
        },
        {
          "id": "redundant_tenant_check",
          "severity": "low",
          "summary": "get_order performs a tenant_id check (row['tenant_id'] != tenant.tenant_id) even when called from list_orders, which already filters by tenant. This is redundant work."
        }
